WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.608
Rewind steps back through whole turns.

2
00:00:01.608 --> 00:00:08.000
This episode is finer-grained: deleting a single reasoning block, a single tool call, or a single tool response, without touching the rest of the chat.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.762
Three controls, all triggered by hovering.

4
00:00:09.762 --> 00:00:12.741
Hover a reasoning block and you get “Delete this thinking from context.

5
00:00:12.741 --> 00:00:15.594
” Hover a tool call and you get “Delete this tool call from context.

6
00:00:15.594 --> 00:00:18.657
” Hover its response and you get “Delete this tool response from context.

7
00:00:18.657 --> 00:00:20.000
” Each targets exactly one item.

8
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.944
Why this exists: a long tool-using session accumulates noisy or flat-out wrong intermediate steps.

9
00:00:23.944 --> 00:00:29.941
That's poisoning the model's context — it keeps referring back to a bad grep or a stale file read — but the thread as a whole is still worth keeping.

10
00:00:29.941 --> 00:00:33.000
Full compaction is too blunt, and starting over throws away everything good.

11
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.418
So surgical deletion beats the two blunt alternatives.

12
00:00:35.418 --> 00:00:45.000
Instead of starting over and losing the whole thread, or running a full compaction that summarizes everything, you remove exactly the items that are wrong or irrelevant, and leave the rest of the context untouched.

13
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.782
And the direct payoff: fewer tokens.

14
00:00:46.782 --> 00:00:55.000
Every deleted reasoning block, tool call, or tool response is context the model no longer has to read on the next turn — a smaller, cleaner prompt, and a cheaper one.

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.013
Gotcha: these controls are disabled while a reply is streaming.

16
00:00:59.013 --> 00:01:05.000
Deletion only applies to settled history — you can't reach in and edit context mid-generation.

17
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Module 16 turns to keeping the app itself current — how updates work, and why a failed signature check gets refused rather than quietly applied.

